 Christopher Layton, A Step Beyond Fear: Building a European Security Community:"}, {"text": "[…] preview of horrific images to come, as the hardware stage of the war yields to the software — or human — stage.", "reference": "1991, New York Magazine, volume 24, number 5, page 33:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(music industry, dated) Recorded music content, as opposed to the hardware necessary for reproducing it.", "tags": ["dated", "uncountable", "usually"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[…] an extremely long playing time is achieved, which might be unnecessary from a software point of view", "reference": "1979 December 6, Toshi T. Doi, Takashi Itoh, Hiroshi Ogawa, “A Long-Play Digital Audio Disk System”, in Journal of the AES, volume 27, number 12, pages 975–981:"}, {"text": "There seems to be a growing trend towards endorsement of the C-DAD system by equipment manufacturers and \"software\" (recorded material) producers around the world.", "reference": "1982 March, Len Feldman, “Digital Audio Disc”, in Radio-Electronics, volume 53, number 3, New York: Gernsback Publications, →ISSN, archived from the original on 09 Mar 2021, page 41:"}, {"text": "Software availability will probably be a significant problem for at least the first few months of the system's commercial life. At present there are only two CD pressing plants in operation, CBS/Sony's in Japan and PolyGram's in Germany.", "reference": "1982 December, David Ranada, “Digital Debut: First impressions of the Compact Disc System”, in Stereo Review, volume 47, number 12, →ISSN, archived from the original on 09 Mar 2021, page 69:"}, {"text": "PLENTIFUL SOFTWARE\nCompact Disc already has support from the recording studios. Many familiar labels appear on these shining silver discs, so that a wide choice of music from the world's leading artistes is available.", "reference": "1984, CD202 Slimline CD Player (Philips advertising brochure), archived from the original on 31 Dec 2025:"}, {"text": "It should be noted that there are industry observers who feel that the whole concept of individually owned music software could become obsolete, and that in the future no digital consumer medium will exist. Instead, the listener will pay for a cable service whereby he can call up musical selections from a vast central electronic memory—a sort of library of music.", "reference": "1986, Daniel Sweeney, Demystifying Compact Discs: A Guide to Digital Audio, 1st edition, Blue Ridge Summit: TAB Books, →ISBN, page 161:"}, {"text": "Anders als bei Videorecordern […] war mit Blick auf die völlige Abhängigkeit von vorgefertigter Musik-Software die Einführung gleich mehrerer neuer Tonträgersysteme nicht zu erwarten.", "reference": "1996, Jürgen K. Lang, Das Compact Disc Digital Audio System, →ISBN, page 37:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(archaic) Soft goods, such as textiles.", "tags": ["archaic", "uncountable", "usually"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "In fact, under the heads of hardware, software, and agriculture, the leading interests of the colony might be said to be grouped.", "reference": "1889, Parliamentary Debates, volume 61, Victoria. Parliament, page 1404:"}, {"text": "Drop into the first emporium you pass, whether of hardware or software, of dry goods or wet goods, and note how comparatively few of the articles displayed represent really original ideas", "reference": "1914, Journal of Pharmaceutical Sciences, volume 3, page 167:"}, {"text": "We have wheat \"kings\" and cotton \"kings\" and coal \"kings\"; railroad \"kings\", stationary engine \"kings\", and Mr Ford; \"kings\" in the realms of hardware, software, sectional bookcases, crime, and canned tomatoes.", "reference": "1927, Ernest Bramah, Max Carrados Mysteries:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["program", "software", "app"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/software", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}